911	/* ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
912	   SCROLLBAR — one Kodely-branded scrollbar, everywhere.
913	   ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
914	   Applied on :root rather than per-container, so every scrollable region in
915	   the product — the sidebar, a modal body, a code panel, a long settings
916	   page — gets it automatically with zero per-component work. A component
917	   that genuinely needs a different treatment (dark-on-dark inside the
918	   builder's code view, say) can still override `scrollbar-color` locally;
919	   this is the default, not a lock.
920	
921	   Firefox uses the two-value `scrollbar-color` property directly — no way to
922	   restyle its shape, only its two colours, which is why the thumb colour
923	   below is chosen to read clearly at Firefox's fixed width too. Chrome,
924	   Safari and Edge use the `::-webkit-scrollbar*` pseudo-elements, which DO
925	   control shape — thinner, rounded, and with a hover state to match the
926	   rest of the product's hover language (border-mid on hover, same as a
927	   ghost button). Both engines are targeted; neither is left on its browser
928	   default, which is what made every scrollable panel look like it belonged
929	   to a different, older product. */
930	:root {
931	  scrollbar-width: thin;
932	  scrollbar-color: var(--border-strong) transparent;
933	}
934	
935	::-webkit-scrollbar {
936	  width: 10px;
937	  height: 10px;
938	}
939	::-webkit-scrollbar-track {
940	  background: transparent;
941	}
942	::-webkit-scrollbar-thumb {
943	  background-color: var(--border-strong);
944	  border-radius: 999px;
945	  /* A transparent border matching the track colour insets the visible thumb
946	     within the scrollbar's own box — the standard trick for a slim rounded
947	     thumb that doesn't touch the edge of a rounded panel it scrolls inside. */
948	  border: 2px solid transparent;
949	  background-clip: padding-box;
950	}
951	::-webkit-scrollbar-thumb:hover {
952	  background-color: var(--brand-ring);
953	}
954	::-webkit-scrollbar-corner {
955	  background: transparent;
956	}
